      "observed": "C3 is a 100 nF capacitor connected between the +5V rail and the RST net, with C3.1 on +5V and C3.2 on RST.",
      "expected": "A reset capacitor for an ATtiny should normally be connected from the reset pin to ground (or otherwise form a standard RC power-on-reset network) so that reset is held low during power-up and reset noise is filtered relative to ground.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "+5V : C2.1(passive) C3.1(passive)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "RST : C3.2(passive) J1.4(Pin_4,passive) J4.5(~{RST},passive) U2.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional) U3.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional)"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "C3 +5V RST 100n"}
      ],
      "impact": "The reset pin is not properly decoupled to ground; this can make MCU power-on reset unreliable and allow reset-line noise to couple to the supply rail, potentially causing unstable or failed startup.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "An anomaly is present: the reset capacitor C3 is tied to +5V instead of ground, which is inconsistent with standard ATtiny reset practice and can affect reliable startup."
}

